   232	`h` has `k<0`. (`Y` is the finite normalisation of `A^2` in `k(S)`, hence
   233	separated and normal; singularities are isolated and unused here.)
   234	
   235	Leading coefficient of `D^j h`. In `K((s))` write `h=alpha s^k + O(s^(k+1))`
   236	with `alpha in K^*`. The principal part `D_prin=c s^(-1) partial_s` sends
   237	`s^k` to `c k s^(k-2)`. Inductively
   238	
   239	```text
   240	D_prin^j h = alpha c^j (prod_(i=0)^(j-1) (k-2i)) s^(k-2j) + higher.
   241	```
   242	
   243	A holomorphic correction is `a(s) partial_s` with `a(0)` finite, hence drops
   244	valuation by at most 1. A residue-field derivation of `K` applied to the
   245	leading coefficient produces a term of valuation `k`, strictly greater than
   246	`k-2`. Any insertion of a non-principal summand therefore cannot cancel the
   247	principal-part leader.
   248	
   249	The product `prod_(i=0)^(j-1)(k-2i)` vanishes iff `k=2i` for some
   250	`0<=i<=j-1`, iff `k` is even and `0<=k<=2(j-1)`. This is impossible for
   251	`k<0`. Characteristic zero is used (no vanishing of the integer `k` itself
   252	as a coefficient). Thus `v(D^j h)=k-2j` exactly, which is (1.15).
   253	
   254	Linear independence. The valuations `k, k-2, k-4, ...` are strictly
   255	decreasing, hence distinct. A `C`-linear relation among the iterates would
   256	have a unique most polar term. Local finiteness of `X_f` on `R` would make
   257	the orbit of `h` finite-dimensional. Contradiction. The same for `X_g`.
   258	
   259	Independence of the automorphism/LND theorem is real: the argument uses the
   260	constant Jacobian (so `X_f(b)=kappa b_g`), the reviewed `(2,1,1)` tame
   261	boundary, affinity of `S`, and separatedness of `Y`. It does not use the
   262	Dubouloz--Palka automorphism formula. The slogan "from the reviewed boundary
   263	packet alone" is slightly tighter than the ingredients: the constant
   264	Jacobian is used, and is displayed in the same section. That is a wording
   265	tightening, not a gap in the proof. Blast radius zero.
   266	
   267	FALLACY-v2 floor/attainment: (1.14) and (1.15) are equalities with displayed
   268	leaders, not floors. Pole identities are applied at the generic point of a
   269	boundary divisor, after the vertex class (tame index two) is checked.
