+# General coordinate-fibre connectedness
+
+## 1. Charge, frozen inputs, and verdict
+
+## 2. Algebraic and geometric setup
+
+Put
+
+```text
+K=Frac(R),                 E_f=C(f),
+pi=(f,g):S->A2,            R=C[A,U,Z]/(U^2-A-A^2Z).
+```
+
+The three frozen inputs have the requested SHA-256 digests
+`2710e90f...e5f5`, `8abde87c...9787`, and `d5fa676a...b97a`, respectively.
+No unlisted source or `jc2-lean` was inspected.
+
+The boundary datum used below must be stated in its exhaustive form.  In the
+reviewed census, `B` is the whole divisorial image of the normalization
+boundary:
+
+```text
+(BC)  pi(Y-S)=B set-theoretically in codimension one.
+```
+
+Equivalently, every prime divisor of `Y-S` maps into the single irreducible
+curve `B`.  This is the content needed from the packet's phrases
+“ramification boundary,” “sole irreducible nonvertical boundary image `B`,”
+and “there are no other finite missing points”; merely knowing that one
+irreducible branch component is called `B` would not suffice.  The cusp in
+the reviewed census makes `B` neither a vertical nor a horizontal line (a
+coordinate line is smooth).  Thus `(BC)` excludes a boundary divisor mapping
+onto either kind of coordinate line.  Cofiniteness of `pi(S)` alone does
+*not* do this: other retained sheets could still cover the generic point of
+such a line.
+
+Since `pi|_S` is etale, `S` is smooth and both `f:S->A1` and `g:S->A1` are
+smooth.  In particular, every scheme fibre of either coordinate is a smooth,
+hence reduced, curve.  For such a fibre, connectedness and irreducibility are
+equivalent: distinct irreducible components of a smooth curve are disjoint.
+
+We use the following standard field form of generic geometric integrality.
+For a dominant map from an integral variety to a smooth curve in
+characteristic zero, its generic fibre is geometrically integral exactly when
+the base function field is algebraically closed in the total function field.
+Once this holds, geometric integrality spreads over a nonempty open subset of
+the base.  Since the base here is `A1`, the exceptional set is finite.
+
