+horizontal line `{g=a}`.  Condition `(BC)` would force that line to equal
+`B`, contrary to the cusp (equivalently, to the charged statement that `B`
+is not horizontal).  Hence
+
+```text
+C(g) is algebraically closed in K,
+```
+
+and the general `g`-fibre is geometrically irreducible and connected.
+
+Nothing in the argument breaks under the interchange.  What must also be
+interchanged is the directional boundary hypothesis: the `f` proof needs
+`B` nonvertical, whereas the `g` proof needs `B` nonhorizontal.  Both are
+available here.  The exceptional-value discussion is likewise symmetric,
+with horizontal tangencies and the `g`-coordinates of the listed special
+sets substituted for their vertical counterparts.
