+### 3.3 Intrinsic primitivity from rationality and units
+
+First, the defining equation gives
+
+```text
+K=C(A,U),                  Z=(U^2-A)/A^2,
+```
+
+so `K` is a rational function field of transcendence degree two.  The curve
+`Tbar_f` is dominated rationally by `P2`; restricting a dominant rational map
+to a general line shows that it is a unirational complex curve.  Hence
+`Tbar_f` has genus zero and is `P1`.
+
+The affine curve `T_f` is `Tbar_f` minus the nonempty finite set over
+`f=infinity`.  After choosing one of those points as infinity,
+
+```text
+O(T_f)=C[t,1/Q(t)]
+```
+
+for a square-free polynomial `Q`; in particular `O(T_f)` is a PID.  As shown
+in §3.2, normality gives `O(T_f) subset R`, and hence a dominant Stein map
+
+```text
+q_f:S->T_f,                  f=tau o q_f.
+```
+
+The exact ring has no nonconstant units.  Indeed,
+
+```text
+R_A=C[A,A^(-1),U],
+```
+
+so a unit of `R` becomes `cA^n` after localization.  At the height-one prime
+`P=(A,U)`, the relation gives `ord_P(A)=2`; a global unit has order zero.
+Thus `n=0` and
+
+```text
+R^*=C^*.                                                     (3.2*)
+```
+
+Now `q_f` is surjective.  If a closed point `d in T_f` were omitted, write
+its principal maximal ideal as `m_d=(w_d)`.  Then `w_d`, viewed in `R`, would
+vanish nowhere on the affine variety `S`, hence would be a unit.  It is
+nonconstant, contradicting (3.2*).  The generic point is hit by dominance,
+so every point of `T_f` is hit.
+
+If `tau:T_f->A1_f` ramified at a finite point `d`, choose
+`s in q_f^(-1)(d)`.  The differential of `tau` at `d` would be zero, and
+
+```text
+d f_s=d(tau o q_f)_s=0.
+```
+
+But `f` is smooth because it is a coordinate projection after the etale map
+`pi|_S`; hence `df_s` never vanishes.  Therefore `tau` has no finite
+ramification.  Section 3.1 shows that a connected cover of degree greater
+than one cannot have this property.  Consequently `delta_f=1`, i.e.
+
+```text
+C(f) is algebraically closed in K.                         (3.3*)
+```
